  "editorReview": {
    "text": "This chronicle of the Cuban Missile Crisis through its paperwork delivers on its core promise. The bureaucratic voice is sustained and effective, framing documents from ExComm tapes to Soviet minutes with a texture that makes the 'paperwork standard' thesis physically tangible, especially in the powerful dismantlement and hotline chapters. However, the narrative drive falters in the middle, betrayed by awkwardly meta chapter titles, and is undercut by prose that occasionally becomes repetitive or mechanical. A more significant reservation concerns the sourcing: the use of uncitable 'family pap",
    "penName": "砚北",
    "title": "History & Social Science Editor",
    "rating": "Editor's notes"
